  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Warming and drying trend through Sunday; cool/cold valley lows\n   with patchy frost.\n\n-  Westerly winds increase this afternoon and Monday with passing\n   disturbances.\n\nMid- and high-level clouds will move through today as a weak \ndisturbance passes along the Canadian border. A few light showers \nor virga may develop this morning as shown by high-resolution \nforecast models, but very little if any rain will reach the \nsurface. Daytime temperatures will warm 5 to 10 degrees compared \nto yesterday, accompanied by a notable afternoon westerly breeze \ngusting between 15 and 25 mph.\n\nOtherwise, high pressure ridging will build over the region, \nbringing a very pleasant weekend. Daytime temperatures will run 10\nto 20 degrees above normal alongside abundant daily sunshine. \nClear skies overnight will promote strong cooling, dropping valley\ntemperatures into the 30s and causing patchy frost in cold-prone \nlocations. Isolated, shallow morning valley fog is possible in \nlow-lying basins, though widespread impacts are unlikely.\n\nA second weak disturbance will slide east through the region \nMonday into Tuesday, weakening the high-pressure ridge and \nincreasing westerly winds. Slight atmospheric instability from \nthis system may support isolated afternoon showers and gustier \nsurface winds. High pressure will rebuild mid-to-late next week, \nmaintaining warm and dry conditions. The first sign of a change \nappears in ensemble models around October 9th or 10th, when a \npotential (60% chance) weak upper-level trough brings cooler \ntemperatures and light showers; however, confidence in exact \ntiming and details remains low. Overall, the primary story over \nthe next two weeks is persistent high pressure, leading to warmer \nand drier than normal conditions. Occasional weak disturbances \nwill knock down the ridge but no significant precipitation \nexpected.",
